13. MASTER + SUB REQUISITION ARCHITECTURE
============================================================

The system must maintain:

MASTER REQUISITION
        |
        +-- LOCATION SUB REQUISITION
        |
        +-- LOCATION SUB REQUISITION
        |
        +-- LOCATION SUB REQUISITION


Example:

Master:

REQ-20260828-015

Backend creates:

REQ-20260828-015/02
Location: Dharamshala Maintenance

REQ-20260828-015/06
Location: Shimla Site

REQ-20260828-015/09
Location: Chandigarh Office


The suffix is generated by backend.

USER DOES NOT ENTER THE SUB-REQUISITION NUMBER.


============================================================
14. SUB REQUISITION LOCATION GROUPING
============================================================

CRITICAL RULE:

Same Master Requisition + Same Location
= Same Sub Requisition.

Example:

09:00 AM:

Cement
Location: Dharamshala Maintenance

Backend creates:

REQ-20260828-015/02


03:00 PM:

Phenyl
Location: Dharamshala Maintenance

Backend must NOT create:

REQ-20260828-015/03

Instead:

Phenyl must be added to:

REQ-20260828-015/02


Therefore:

REQ-20260828-015/02

Cement
Phenyl
Gloves


All belong to the same location.


============================================================
15. NEW LOCATION
============================================================

If a new location is used in the same Master Requisition:

Backend creates a new Sub Requisition.

Example:

Master:
REQ-20260828-015

Existing:
REQ-20260828-015/02
Dharamshala Maintenance

New location:
Shimla Site

Create:

REQ-20260828-015/06


The exact suffix allocation mechanism must be centralized
and configurable.

Never create duplicate sub requisitions for the same
Master Requisition + Location combination.


